Debug.Assert(OLD(_items.Length) + y * 2 <= RET);
</code>
Here `y` represents the number of elements to add. Hence in the given code, when `y` elements are added to the list, the capacity of the list can increase by `y * 2`. The assertion checks whether the length of the internal array increases by at least `y * 2` which implies the list's capacity increases dynamically as elements are added.--------------------
Debug.Assert(OLD(x) + y == RET);
</code>

Explanation:
The assert statement is used to check the correctness of the program. It is like an assertion, which is a true statement. The Assertion in the code checks the value of x after the execution of the function. If the value is not as expected, the code execution will stop and an error message will be shown. In this case, the Assertion checks if the value of `x` increased by `y` equals the return value.

Here, the return value is `x` which is the sum of `x` and `y`. Therefore, if the `Sum` function works correctly, the assertion condition will be true.--------------------
Debug.Assert(RET - y = x);
